4.2 Spectral Analysis L22:     3. cite12†4.3 Structural Limitations of LoRA L23:   6. cite13†5 Regularized Low-Rank Adaptation L24:     1. cite14†5.1 Clean-strengthened Regularization L25:     2. cite15†5.2 Trigger-insensitive Regularization L26:     3. cite16†5.3 Post-training Spectral Rescaling L27:     4. cite17†5.4 Empirical Validation L28:   7. cite18†6 Experiments L29:     1. cite19†6.1 Main Results L30:     2. cite20†6.2 Ablation Studies L31:   8. cite21†7 Conclusion L32:   9. cite22†References L33:   10. cite23†A Appendix L34:     1. cite24†A.1 Implementation Details L35:     2. cite25†A.2 Proof of Proposition  L36:     3. cite26†A.3 More Experiments L37: cite27†License: CC BY 4.0†info.arxiv.org L38: \nL39: arXiv:2601.06305v1 [cs.CL] 09 Jan 2026\nL40: # Why LoRA Fails to Forget: Regularized Low-Rank Adaptation Against Backdoors in Language Models\nL41: \nL42: Hoang-Chau Luong Lingwei Chen Affiliation: Golisano College of Computing and Information Sciences Affiliation: Rochester Institute of Technology Affiliation: Rochester, NY, United States Affiliation: cl6300@rit.edu, lwcics@rit.edu\nL43: ###### Abstract\nL44: Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA) is widely used for parameter-efficient fine-tuning of large language models, but it is notably ineffective at removing backdoor behaviors from poisoned pretrained models when fine-tuning on clean dataset. Contrary to the common belief that this weakness is caused primarily by low rank, we show that LoRA’s vulnerability is fundamentally spectral.\nL45: Our analysis identifies two key factors: LoRA updates (i) possess insufficient spectral strength, with singular values far below those of pretrained weights, and (ii) exhibit unfavorable spectral alignment, weakly matching clean-task directions while retaining overlap with trigger-sensitive subspaces. We further establish a critical scaling threshold beyond which LoRA can theoretically suppress trigger-induced activations, and we show empirically that standard LoRA rarely reaches this regime.\nL46: We introduce Regularized Low-Rank Adaptation (RoRA), which improves forgetting by increasing spectral strength and correcting alignment through clean-strengthened regularization, trigger-insensitive constraints, and post-training spectral rescaling. Experiments across multiple NLP benchmarks and attack settings show that RoRA substantially reduces attack success rates while maintaining clean accuracy.\nL47: ## 1 Introduction\nL48: Large language models (LLMs) have achieved remarkable success across a wide range of natural language processing (NLP) tasks (cite28†Brown et al., 2020 ; cite29†Raffel et al., 2020 ; cite30†Devlin et al., 2019 ).\nL49: However, the rapid growth in model size has rendered full fine-tuning increasingly expensive (cite31†Ding et al., 2023 ), motivating the development of parameter-efficient fine-tuning (PEFT) methods that adapt pretrained models with substantially reduced both computational and memory overhead (cite32†Houlsby et al., 2019 ; cite33†Lester et al., 2021 ; cite34†Hu et al., 2022a ).\nL335:   * Liu et al. (2019) Y. Liu, M. Ott, N. Goyal, J. Du, M. Joshi, D. Chen, O. Levy, M. Lewis, L. Zettlemoyer, and V. Stoyanov Roberta: a robustly optimized bert pretraining approach. arXiv preprint arXiv:1907.11692. Cited by: cite87†§6 .\nL336:   * Maas et al. (2011) A. Maas, R. E. Daly, P. T. Pham, D. Huang, A. Y. Ng, and C. Potts Learning word vectors for sentiment analysis. In Proceedings of the 49th annual meeting of the association for computational linguistics: Human language technologies, Cited by: cite92†§6 .\nL337:   * Mangrulkar et al. (2022) S. Mangrulkar, S. Gugger, L. Debut, Y. Belkada, S. Paul, B. Bossan, and M. Tietz PEFT: state-of-the-art parameter-efficient fine-tuning methods. Note: cite95†https://github.com/huggingface/peft†github.com Cited by: cite96†§A.1 .\nL338:   * McCloskey and Cohen (1989) M. McCloskey and N. J. Cohen Catastrophic interference in connectionist networks: the sequential learning problem. In Psychology of learning and motivation, Cited by: cite90†§4.1 .\nL339:   * Meng et al. (2024) F. Meng, Z. Wang, and M. Zhang Pissa: principal singular values and singular vectors adaptation of large language models. Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems. Cited by: cite83†§A.3 , cite84†§6 .\nL340:   * Minh and Tuan (2022) D. N. Minh and L. A. Tuan Textual manifold-based defense against natural language adversarial examples. In Proceedings of the 2022 conference on empirical methods in natural language processing, Cited by: cite85†§2 .\nL341:   * Pang and Lee (2005) B. Pang and L. Lee Seeing stars: exploiting class relationships for sentiment categorization with respect to rating scales. arXiv preprint cs/0506075. Cited by: cite92†§6 .\nL342:   * Qi et al. (2021a) F. Qi, Y. Chen, M. Li, Y. Yao, Z. Liu, and M. Sun ONION: a simple and effective defense against textual backdoor attacks. In Proceedings of the 2021 Conference on Empirical Methods in Natural Language Processing, Cited by: cite97†§2 , cite84†§6 .\nL343:   * Qi et al. (2021b) F. Qi, M. Li, Y. Chen, Z. Zhang, Z. Liu, Y. Wang, and M. Sun Hidden killer: invisible textual backdoor attacks with syntactic trigger. In Proceedings of the 59th Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics and the 11th International Joint Conference on Natural Language Processing (Volume 1: Long Papers), C. Zong, F. Xia, W. Li, and R. Navigli (Eds.), Cited by: cite85†§2 , cite97†§2 , cite84†§6 .\nL462: Dataset Pair  | LLaMA  | RoBERTa  | BERT\nL463: IMDB $\\rightarrow$ SST-2  | 50.03  | 79.09  | 76.11\nL464: MR $\\rightarrow$ CR  | 85.94  | 88.39  | 82.32\nL465: \nL466: Table 6: Clean accuracy (%) on the SST-2, CR test sets obtained by pretrained models without fine-tuning, InSent attack.\nL467: LoRA’s weak representations. Table cite110†6 reports additional clean-accuracy results for poisoned pretraining under the InSent attack. For the MR$\\rightarrow$CR pair, clean accuracy remains consistently above 82%. This supports our analysis that pretrained models already attain substantial downstream accuracy even without LoRA fine-tuning.\nL468: \nL469: Experimental support, please cite111†view the build logs for errors. Generated by cite112†L A T E xml†math.nist.gov .\nL470: ## Instructions for reporting errors\nL471: \nL472: We are continuing to improve HTML versions of papers, and your feedback helps enhance accessibility and mobile support. To report errors in the HTML that will help us improve conversion and rendering, choose any of the methods listed below:\nL473: \nL474:   * Click the \"Report Issue\" () button, located in the page header.\nL475: \nL476: Tip: You can select the relevant text first, to include it in your report.\nL477: Our team has already identified cite113†the following issues†github.com . We appreciate your time reviewing and reporting rendering errors we may not have found yet. Your efforts will help us improve the HTML versions for all readers, because disability should not be a barrier to accessing research. Thank you for your continued support in championing open access for all.\nL478: Have a free development cycle? Help support accessibility at arXiv! Our collaborators at LaTeXML maintain a cite114†list of packages that need conversion†github.com , and welcome cite115†developer contributions†github.com .\nL479: \nL480: We gratefully acknowledge support from our major funders, cite116†member institutions†info.arxiv.org , , and all contributors.\nL481: cite117†About†info.arxiv.org · cite118†Help†info.arxiv.org · cite119†Contact†info.arxiv.org · cite120†Subscribe†info.arxiv.org · cite121†Copyright†info.arxiv.org · cite122†Privacy†info.arxiv.org · cite123†Accessibility†info.arxiv.org · cite124†Operational Status (opens in new tab)†status.arxiv.org L482: \nL483: Major funding support from\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPRPO: Aligning Process Reward with Outcome Reward in Policy Optimization (https://arxiv.org/html/2601.07182v1)\nciteturn26952view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn26941view5\",\"pattern\":\"github\"}); Total lines: 494\nL0: ##### Report GitHub Issue\nL1: \nL2: [Button: ×]\nL3: \nL4: Title: [Input: Enter title]\nL5: \nL6: Content selection saved. Describe the issue below:\nL7: \nL8: Description:\nL9: \nL10: [Button: Submit without GitHub] [Button: Submit in GitHub]\nL11: \nL12: cite0†Back to arXiv L13: \nL14: cite1†Why HTML?†info.arxiv.org cite2†Report Issue cite3†Back to Abstract cite4†Download PDF L15:   1. cite5†Abstract L16:   2. cite6†1 Introduction L17:   3. cite7†2 Related Work L18:     1. cite8†2.1 Critic-Free Policy Optimization L19:     2. cite9†2.2 Process Reward Models L20:   4. cite10†3 PRPO L21:     1. cite11†3.1 Semantic-Based Sequence Segmentation L22:     2. cite12†3.2 Aligning Outcome-level and Process-level Reward L23:       1. cite13†Premature Collapse in Process-Only Reward L24:       2. cite14†Distribution Alignment L25:   5. cite15†4 Experiments L26:   6. cite16†5 Conclusion L27:   7. cite17†6 Limitations L28:   8. cite18†7 Potential Risks L29:   9. cite19†References L30:   10. cite20†A Possible Mathematical Reason for Premature Collapse in Process-Only Reward L31:   11. cite21†B Entropy Based Segmentation Strategy L32:   12. cite22†C Early Stop Point for Each Trainig L33:     1. cite23†C.1 Qwen2.5-Math-1.5B L34:     2. cite24†C.2 Qwen2.5-Math-7B L35:   13. cite25†D Disclosure of use of AI Tools L36:   14. cite26†E Disclosure of license of the artifacts we used L37: cite27†License: CC BY 4.0†info.arxiv.org L38: \nL39: arXiv:2601.07182v1 [cs.LG] 12 Jan 2026\nL40: # PRPO: Aligning Process Reward with Outcome Reward in Policy Optimization\nL41: Ruiyi DingShanghai University,Shanghai, China Email: dingruiyi@shu.edu.cn    Yongxuan LvUSTC,Hefei, China Email: lvyongxuan@ustc.edu.cn    Xianhui MengUSTC,Hefei, China Email: mengxianhui@ustc.edu.cn    Jiahe SongShanghai Jiaotong University,Shanghai, China Email: songjiahe@sjtu.edu.cn    Chao Wang ^{*}Shanghai University,Shanghai, China Email: cwang@shu.edu.cn    Chen Jiang ^{*} Email: jiangchen@sais.org.cn    AI Institute Email: chengyuan@sais.org.cn    SAISFudan University,Shanghai, China    Yuan Cheng ^{*}    AI Institute    SAISFudan University,Shanghai, China\nL42: ###### Abstract\nL43: Policy optimization for large language models often suffers from sparse reward signals in multi-step reasoning tasks. Critic-free methods like GRPO assign a single normalized outcome reward to all tokens, providing limited guidance for intermediate reasoning . While Process Reward Models (PRMs) offer dense feedback, they risk premature collapse when used alone, as early low-reward tokens can drive policies toward truncated outputs.\nL44: We introduce Process Relative Policy Optimization (PRPO), which combines outcome reliability with process-level guidance in a critic-free framework. PRPO segments reasoning sequences based on semantic clues, normalizes PRM scores into token-level advantages, and aligns their distribution with outcome advantages through location-parameter shift.\nL45: On MATH500, PRPO improves Qwen2.5-Math-1.5B accuracy from 61.2% to 64.4% over GRPO using only eight rollouts and no value network, demonstrating efficient fine-grained credit assignment within critic-free optimization.\nL46: cite28†Image: Refer to caption Figure 1: Comparison between outcome-level and process-level reward in RLHF.\nL47: ## 1 Introduction\nL48: Reinforcement learning (RL) has become a central paradigm for aligning large language models (LLMs) with human objectives, particularly in tasks that require complex reasoning, long-horizon planning, and structured decision-making. Early RL-based alignment methods, such as reinforcement learning from human feedback (RLHF), primarily rely on sequence-level rewards that evaluate only the final generated output.\nL452: ## Appendix E Disclosure of license of the artifacts we used\nL453: \nL454: The artifacts we used are all open-source, and we used the following licenses:\nL455: \nL456:   * •\nL457: \nL458: The model Qwen2.5-Math-1.5B is released under the Apache License 2.0\nL459: \nL460:   * •\nL461: \nL462: The model Qwen2.5-Math-7B is released under the Apache License 2.0\nL463: \nL464:   * •\nL465: \nL466: The model Qwen2.5-Math-PRM-7B is released under the Apache License 2.0\nL467: \nL468:   * •\nL469: \nL470: The dataset MATH is released under the MIT License\nL471: \nL472:   * •\nL473: \nL474: The training framework VeRL is released under the Apache License 2.0\nL475: We strictly follow the license of the open-source artifacts we used.\nL476: \nL477: The data of AMC 2023, AIME 2025, and AIME 2024 are extracted from open website Art of Problem Solving (cite56†Art of Problem Solving, 2025a ). We strictly follow the terms of use of this website and did not use the data obtained from the website for any commercial purpose.\nL478: \nL479: Experimental support, please cite90†view the build logs for errors. Generated by cite91†L A T E xml†math.nist.gov .\nL480: ## Instructions for reporting errors\nL481: \nL482: We are continuing to improve HTML versions of papers, and your feedback helps enhance accessibility and mobile support. To report errors in the HTML that will help us improve conversion and rendering, choose any of the methods listed below:\nL483: \nL484:   * Click the \"Report Issue\" () button, located in the page header.\nL485: \nL486: Tip: You can select the relevant text first, to include it in your report.\nL487: Our team has already identified cite92†the following issues†github.com . We appreciate your time reviewing and reporting rendering errors we may not have found yet. Your efforts will help us improve the HTML versions for all readers, because disability should not be a barrier to accessing research. Thank you for your continued support in championing open access for all.\nL488: Have a free development cycle? Help support accessibility at arXiv! Our collaborators at LaTeXML maintain a cite93†list of packages that need conversion†github.com , and welcome cite94†developer contributions†github.com .\nL489: \nL490: We gratefully acknowledge support from our major funders, cite95†member institutions†info.arxiv.org , , and all contributors.\nL491: cite96†About†info.arxiv.org · cite97†Help†info.arxiv.org · cite98†Contact†info.arxiv.org · cite99†Subscribe†info.arxiv.org · cite100†Copyright†info.arxiv.org · cite101†Privacy†info.arxiv.org · cite102†Accessibility†info.arxiv.org · cite103†Operational Status (opens in new tab)†status.arxiv.org L492: \nL493: Major funding support from\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOn the Fallacy of Global Token Perplexity in Spoken Language Model Evaluation (https://arxiv.org/html/2601.06329v1)\nciteturn26952view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn26941view2\",\"pattern\":\"Limitations\"}); Total lines: 588\nL204: The case of SpiritLM is markedly different from Llama-mimi. In Table cite90†2 , HuBERT tokens are contributing to acoustic tasks more than the other tokens combined, despite being labeled as a \"semantic\" token type. HuBERT Shapley values drop under localization, suggesting that a non-trivial portion of the acoustic information they encode is only elicitable through long-range dependencies.\nL205: Surprisingly, normalization further reduces HuBERT’s Shapley values, suggesting large volatility in non-acoustic information. The advantage breakdown confirms that alternative methods do not yield a greater overall gain, and the perceived benefit of amplifying pitch contributions is practically negligible, as HuBERT tokens remains equally crucial for capturing acoustic information.\nL206: As we uncover the role of HuBERT tokens in Spirit-LM expressive, it becomes more clear why models that adopt HuBERT tokens ($\\phi_{H}$) constantly has continuation underperforming global perplexity (Figure cite85†3 ). The performance during global perplexity of HuBERT modeling depend greatly on long range dependency to identify the correctness, but continuation content happens at each instance and cannot rely on future information.\nL207: Our proposed evaluations penalizes this behavior in agreement with continuation results measured by MOS scores.\nL208: ## 6 Conclusion\nL209: In this work, we revisit the use of global token perplexity for evaluating SLMs. We introduce a variety of likelihood- and generation-based evaluation methods, that highlight localization and normalization to better reflect key characteristics of speech. Correlations with MOS show that our proposed methods align more closely with human perception.\nL210: Re-evaluating SLMs with our proposed methods shows that the previously best-performing model achieves gains that close 83% of the gap to the human topline on SALMon. Together, these findings reshape the SLM performance landscape and establish a new evaluation paradigm for future studies.\nL211: ## Limitations\nL212: We propose novel evaluation methods as alternatives to conventional global token perplexity. However, since these methods are still applied to existing benchmarks, their scope remains inherently constrained by the limitations of those benchmarks. For instance, SALMon does not systematically probe compounded variations (e.g., speaker changes under noisy background conditions), which restricts our ability to characterize SLM performance in such settings—even when using our improved evaluators.\nL213: ## References\nL214:   * An et al. (2024) K. An, Q. Chen, C. Deng, Z. Du, C. 